(window.ICQ = window.ICQ || {});
(window.ICQ['ct-genai'] = window.ICQ['ct-genai'] || []).push(
  { id:'c2-001', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.1', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'A tester writes the sentence “The test object is the money-transfer function of a mobile banking app, release 3.1.” as part of a structured prompt. Which prompt component does this sentence mainly represent?', opts:['Role', 'Instruction', 'Context', 'Constraints'], ans:[2], exp:'Context provides the background the model needs, including details about the test object and the functionality to be tested (Syllabus 2.1.1). The sentence does not define a persona (role), does not state a task to perform (instruction) and does not restrict how the task is carried out (constraints).' },
  { id:'c2-002', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.1', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'A prompt for a REST API test task contains the sentence: “Cover only the endpoints listed above and do not invent business rules that are not in the specification.” Which component does it mainly represent?', opts:['Constraints', 'Output format', 'Role', 'Input data'], ans:[0], exp:'Constraints outline restrictions or special considerations the LLM must adhere to and specify how the instructions are applied to the input data (Syllabus 2.1.1). The sentence neither describes the structure of the response (output format), nor a persona (role), nor supplies material such as the specification itself (input data).' },
  { id:'c2-003', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.1', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'What is the main purpose of the role component, for example “You are an experienced test automation engineer”?', opts:['It supplies the user stories and code that the model must analyze', 'It restricts the model to a fixed number of test cases per answer', 'It specifies the table layout in which the model must return its results', 'It defines the perspective the model should take, so that it can determine its responsibilities and adopt a suitable tone or approach'], ans:[3], exp:'The role defines the perspective or persona of the model, helping it determine its responsibilities and tone (Syllabus 2.1.1). Supplying user stories and code is the job of input data, restrictions are constraints, and the response layout is the output format.' },
  { id:'c2-004', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.1', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Which of the following is an example of input data in a structured prompt for a software test task?', opts:['“You are an experienced test manager in a healthcare project.”', '“Return the results as a table with the columns ID, Steps and Expected Result.”', '“Do not suggest tests for features outside this release.”', 'A pasted user story together with its acceptance criteria'], ans:[3], exp:'Input data is the information needed to perform the task, such as user stories, acceptance criteria, screenshots, code or existing test cases (Syllabus 2.1.1). The first option is a role, the second an output format and the third a constraint.' },
  { id:'c2-005', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.1', k:'K2', type:'multi', q:'Which TWO of the following are components of a structured prompt for software testing? (Choose TWO answers.)', opts:['Role', 'Temperature setting', 'Output format', 'Model version', 'Training data'], ans:[0,2], exp:'The six components are role, context, instruction, input data, constraints and output format (Syllabus 2.1.1). Temperature setting, model version and training data are properties of the model or its configuration, not components of the prompt structure.' },
  { id:'c2-006', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.1', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Which of the following statements about the components of a structured prompt are correct?\ni. Instructions should be clear, imperative and concise and include the task description and relevant requirements.\nii. Constraints help to specify how the instructions should be applied to the input data.\niii. Context contains only the persona the model should adopt, such as tester or test manager.', opts:['i and ii only', 'i and iii only', 'ii and iii only', 'i, ii and iii'], ans:[0], exp:'Statements i and ii restate Syllabus 2.1.1 on instructions and constraints. Statement iii is wrong: the persona is the role, whereas context is background information about the test object, the functionality to be tested and other relevant circumstances.' },
  { id:'c2-007', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.1', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Which component is missing from the following prompt?\n“You are a test analyst working on the password-reset function of a mobile banking app. Derive test conditions from the user story below. User story: ‘As a customer, I want to reset my password with an SMS code so that I can regain access.’ Present the conditions as a numbered list.”', opts:['Role', 'Input data', 'Constraints', 'Output format'], ans:[2], exp:'The prompt has a role (test analyst), context (password-reset function of a mobile banking app), an instruction (derive test conditions), input data (the user story) and an output format (numbered list). No restriction on how the task is applied is given, so constraints are missing (Syllabus 2.1.1).' },
  { id:'c2-008', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.1', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Which statement best describes the output format component of a structured prompt?', opts:['It outlines restrictions the model must observe when applying the instructions to the input data', 'It denotes the expected format, structure or characteristics of the response, for example a table with defined columns', 'It provides background about the test object and the functionality to be tested', 'It contains the information needed to perform the task, such as code or existing test cases'], ans:[1], exp:'Output specifications denote the expected format, structure or characteristics of the response and shape the LLM output (Syllabus 2.1.1). The other options describe constraints, context and input data respectively.' },
  { id:'c2-009', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.2', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'A tester splits the test case creation for an airline booking story into three prompts: first list test conditions, then review and correct them manually, then derive test cases from the corrected conditions. Which technique is being applied?', opts:['Prompt chaining', 'Few-shot prompting', 'Meta prompting', 'Zero-shot prompting'], ans:[0], exp:'Prompt chaining breaks a task into a series of intermediate steps whose results are checked and refined before the next prompt (Syllabus 2.1.2). No examples are supplied (not few-shot), the LLM is not asked to create prompts (not meta prompting), and zero-shot would be a single prompt without examples.' },
  { id:'c2-010', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.2', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'A prompt for generating test cases for a login form contains exactly one fully worked sample: a short input description and the expected test case. Which technique does this prompt use?', opts:['One-shot prompting', 'Zero-shot prompting', 'Few-shot prompting', 'Meta prompting'], ans:[0], exp:'One-shot prompting provides one example that demonstrates the expected result for a given input (Syllabus 2.1.2). Zero-shot uses no example, few-shot contains more than one example, and meta prompting uses the LLM to generate or refine prompts.' },
  { id:'c2-011', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.2', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'A tester is unsure how to write an effective prompt for generating boundary value tests for a date field. The tester asks the LLM to draft a suitable prompt, then evaluates and refines it together with the LLM. Which technique is this?', opts:['Prompt chaining', 'Meta prompting', 'One-shot prompting', 'Zero-shot prompting'], ans:[1], exp:'Meta prompting leverages the LLM\'s ability to generate or refine its own prompts in an iterative cycle, and allows the tester to co-create the prompt with the LLM (Syllabus 2.1.2). Chaining splits a task into checked steps, and one-shot and zero-shot concern how many examples a prompt contains.' },
  { id:'c2-012', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.2', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'What distinguishes zero-shot prompting from few-shot prompting?', opts:['Zero-shot gives exactly one example, while few-shot gives several examples', 'Zero-shot results are checked at each step by the tester, while few-shot results are checked only at the end', 'Zero-shot prompts are written by the LLM itself, while few-shot prompts are written by the tester', 'Zero-shot gives no example and relies on the model\'s pre-existing knowledge, while few-shot gives more than one example'], ans:[3], exp:'Zero-shot prompting relies on pre-existing knowledge without examples, whereas few-shot prompts contain more than one example to consolidate the desired behavior (Syllabus 2.1.2). One example is one-shot, step-wise checking is characteristic of chaining, and LLM-written prompts relate to meta prompting.' },
  { id:'c2-013', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.2', k:'K2', type:'multi', q:'Which TWO statements about prompt chaining are correct? (Choose TWO answers.)', opts:['It requires the whole task to be solved in one single prompt', 'The result of each step is checked and refined, manually or automatically, before the next prompt is sent', 'It relies on the LLM writing all prompts of the chain itself', 'It is particularly useful for complicated tasks that need decomposition into subtasks', 'Intermediate results may only be checked automatically, never by a human'], ans:[1,3], exp:'Syllabus 2.1.2 states that chaining decomposes a task into several prompts, that each result is manually or automatically checked before proceeding, and that it suits complicated tasks. A single prompt is the opposite of chaining, LLM-written prompts describe meta prompting, and human checking of intermediate results is explicitly allowed.' },
  { id:'c2-014', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.2', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Which of the following statements about prompting techniques are correct?\ni. Prompt chaining breaks a task into intermediate steps, and each result is checked and refined before the next step.\nii. Meta prompting uses the ability of the LLM to generate or refine prompts, which the tester then evaluates.\niii. Few-shot prompting means sending the same prompt several times and keeping the best response.', opts:['i and iii only', 'i and ii only', 'ii and iii only', 'i, ii and iii'], ans:[1], exp:'Statements i and ii correctly describe chaining and meta prompting (Syllabus 2.1.2). Statement iii is wrong: few-shot prompting means providing more than one example inside the prompt, not repeating a prompt.' },
  { id:'c2-015', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.2', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Which of the following is an advantage of meta prompting?', opts:['It guarantees that generated prompts never need to be reviewed by the tester', 'It forces a human to verify every intermediate output of a multi-step task', 'It reduces the manual effort of designing effective prompts by using the LLM\'s knowledge about optimized prompts', 'It makes examples unnecessary for every kind of test task'], ans:[2], exp:'Meta prompting optimizes prompt quality by exploiting the LLM\'s knowledge about good prompts and reduces manual prompt design effort; the tester still evaluates and refines the generated prompts (Syllabus 2.1.2). Verification of each step is a feature of chaining, and the claim that examples are never needed is not made for any technique.' },
  { id:'c2-016', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.2', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Which of the following prompts is an example of meta prompting?', opts:['“Generate ten test cases for the login page of our web shop.”', '“I need a prompt that makes an LLM find anomalies in the nightly logs of a payment service. Draft it and ask me questions where you need more information.”', '“Input ‘Age 17’ gives ‘Reject’. Input ‘Age 18’ gives ‘Accept’. Now classify ‘Age 65’.”', '“Step 1 only: list the ambiguities in the story below. I will check them before you continue.”'], ans:[1], exp:'In meta prompting the LLM is asked to create or refine a prompt that the tester then evaluates (Syllabus 2.1.2). The first alternative is a plain zero-shot request, the third is few-shot prompting with examples, and the last one is the first step of a prompt chain.' },
  { id:'c2-017', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.3', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Who typically defines the system prompt of a testing chatbot, and is it normally visible to the chatbot\'s user?', opts:['It is defined by the developer or tester and is not visible or editable by the chatbot\'s user in most interfaces', 'It is typed by the chatbot\'s user in every message and is always visible', 'It is generated randomly by the LLM for each answer and is visible in every interface', 'It is defined by the developer but changes with each message and is always editable by the user'], ans:[0], exp:'The system prompt is typically defined by the developer or tester to guide the overall behavior of the LLM and is not visible or editable by the user in most interfaces (Syllabus 2.1.3). The other options describe user prompts or invent behavior.' },
  { id:'c2-018', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.3', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Which of the following texts is best suited as a user prompt rather than as a system prompt?', opts:['“You are an ISTQB-aligned testing assistant. Always answer formally and avoid speculation.”', '“Generate boundary value test cases for the field ‘Quantity’ (valid range 1–99) and show them in a table.”', '“In every answer, keep responses concise and refuse to give legal advice.”', '“For the whole session, follow the naming rules of our company test case template.”'], ans:[1], exp:'A user prompt is the actual task or question of the chatbot\'s user and changes with each interaction (Syllabus 2.1.3). The other three are rules for the whole conversation (persona, tone, domain rules), which belong in the system prompt.' },
  { id:'c2-019', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.3', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Which of the following statements about system prompts and user prompts are correct?\ni. The system prompt stays constant throughout the interaction session.\nii. The user prompt changes with each interaction and forms the immediate context for each response.\niii. After the first answer, the LLM ignores the system prompt and considers only the current user prompt.', opts:['ii and iii only', 'i and iii only', 'i and ii only', 'i, ii and iii'], ans:[2], exp:'Statements i and ii are stated in Syllabus 2.1.3. Statement iii is wrong: the LLM generates its responses by considering both the unchanging system prompt and the current user prompt together.' },
  { id:'c2-020', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.3', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'A test team deploys an internal chatbot. Whatever a tester asks, every answer must be formal, aligned with ISTQB practices and free of speculation. Where should this requirement best be placed?', opts:['In the system prompt, which is set once at the start of the conversation', 'In every user prompt, because system prompts cannot contain constraints', 'In the output format of each user prompt, because tone belongs to the output format only', 'In a separate chain step after each answer, because system prompts only hold input data'], ans:[0], exp:'The system prompt sets the rules for the entire conversation (behavior, tone, domain rules) and is set once, followed by successive user prompts (Syllabus 2.1.3). System prompts may contain role, context and constraints, so the claims in the other options are incorrect.' },
  { id:'c2-021', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.3', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Which parts of a structured prompt does a system prompt typically contain?', opts:['Only the input data of the current task, such as the user story being analyzed', 'Only the instruction for the current task, because system prompts are task-specific', 'Only the output format, because role and constraints belong in the user prompt', 'Role, context and constraints, possibly with general instructions such as the expected output'], ans:[3], exp:'A system prompt may contain parts of a structured prompt such as role, context and constraints, and may also include general instructions, for instance about the expected output (Syllabus 2.1.3). Task-specific input data and instructions are given in the user prompt.' },
  { id:'c2-022', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.3', k:'K2', type:'multi', q:'Which TWO statements correctly describe a user prompt? (Choose TWO answers.)', opts:['It is typically not visible or editable by the chatbot\'s user', 'It sets the rules for the entire conversation', 'It changes with each interaction', 'It is set once at the start and stays constant', 'It represents the actual input or question of the chatbot\'s user'], ans:[2,4], exp:'The user prompt is the actual input or question of the user and changes with every interaction (Syllabus 2.1.3). Invisibility to the user, setting rules for the whole conversation and staying constant are properties of the system prompt.' },
  { id:'c2-023', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.1.3', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Which description reflects the typical usage of system and user prompts in a chat session?', opts:['The user prompt is set once at the start, and a new system prompt is sent for each question', 'Each user prompt replaces the system prompt, which is then deleted from the conversation', 'The system prompt is set once at the start, then successive user prompts follow, and the LLM considers both together', 'The system prompt is used only for the first answer, later answers rely on user prompts alone'], ans:[2], exp:'Syllabus 2.1.3 describes setting the system prompt once at the beginning and sending successive user prompts, with the response generated from the unchanging system prompt and the current user prompt together. The other options reverse or misstate this.' },
  { id:'c2-024', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.1', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'A team generates 50 automated test scripts for a web shop with GenAI. In the test environment, 41 of them run as is, without syntax errors or output format problems, and the team calculates 41/50. Which metric is being calculated?', opts:['Recall', 'Diversity', 'Execution success rate', 'Time efficiency'], ans:[2], exp:'Execution success rate measures the proportion of generated test artefacts that can be executed successfully as is in the test environment (Syllabus 2.3.1). Recall concerns identifying all relevant instances, diversity concerns variety, and time efficiency concerns time saved.' },
  { id:'c2-025', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.1', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'A tester measures that GenAI produced 30 test cases in 4 minutes, while a colleague needed 90 minutes to write equivalent test cases manually. Which metric does this comparison express?', opts:['Accuracy', 'Precision', 'Time efficiency', 'Execution success rate'], ans:[2], exp:'Time efficiency evaluates the time saved compared to manual test effort, for example the AI generation time versus the human creation time (Syllabus 2.3.1). Accuracy, precision and execution success rate assess correctness or executability, not time.' },
  { id:'c2-026', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.1', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'While reviewing GenAI test cases for a ticket-booking app, a tester finds that 20 of them differ only in the customer name and none explores edge cases. Which metric is rated poorly?', opts:['Time efficiency', 'Execution success rate', 'Accuracy', 'Diversity'], ans:[3], exp:'Diversity expresses whether a wide range of inputs and scenarios is covered while avoiding repetition, including user behaviors and edge cases (Syllabus 2.3.1). The described problem is repetition and missing edge cases, not speed, executability or overall correctness.' },
  { id:'c2-027', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.1', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'A log file contains 40 known anomalous entries. The LLM flags 34 of them. The ratio 34/40 expresses the ability of the model to identify all relevant instances in the dataset. Which metric is this?', opts:['Diversity', 'Recall', 'Execution success rate', 'Relevance and contextual fit'], ans:[1], exp:'Recall measures the ability of a model to identify all relevant instances within a dataset (Syllabus 2.3.1), which is what the ratio of found to existing anomalies expresses. Diversity, execution success rate and relevance and contextual fit address variety, executability and appropriateness for a context.' },
  { id:'c2-028', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.1', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'A tester notices that GenAI test cases for an insurance claims portal use terminology and rules from a retail shop and contradict the supplied requirements. Which metric is mainly affected?', opts:['Time efficiency', 'Execution success rate', 'Diversity', 'Relevance and contextual fit'], ans:[3], exp:'Relevance and contextual fit determine whether output is applicable and appropriate for the context, e.g. consistent with the test basis and the domain-specific requirements (Syllabus 2.3.1). Speed, executability and variety are not the issue described.' },
  { id:'c2-029', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.1', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Why should metrics for evaluating GenAI results on test tasks be based on statistically relevant data?', opts:['Because the training data of the LLM must be statistically representative of the project', 'Because GenAI is non-deterministic, so a single output may not represent its typical behavior', 'Because a manual review of a few outputs is not permitted for GenAI results', 'Because automated comparison with a reference is unreliable below a fixed number of outputs'], ans:[1], exp:'Given the non-deterministic nature of GenAI, the metrics must be based on statistically relevant data (Syllabus 2.3.1). The syllabus does not forbid manual reviews, does not set a minimum number of outputs for automated comparison and does not tie the requirement to training data.' },
  { id:'c2-030', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.1', k:'K2', type:'multi', q:'Which TWO of the following are metrics for evaluating GenAI results on test tasks? (Choose TWO answers.)', opts:['Recall', 'Defect density', 'Cyclomatic complexity', 'Time efficiency', 'Test environment availability'], ans:[0,3], exp:'Recall and time efficiency are among the seven metrics named in Syllabus 2.3.1 (accuracy, precision, recall, relevance and contextual fit, diversity, execution success rate, time efficiency). Defect density, cyclomatic complexity and test environment availability are not part of that list.' },
  { id:'c2-031', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.1', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'A tester compares GenAI-generated test cases for a loan calculator with expert-written test cases and the requirements in order to rate their overall correctness. Which metric is applied?', opts:['Diversity', 'Time efficiency', 'Accuracy', 'Execution success rate'], ans:[2], exp:'Accuracy measures the overall correctness of the generated output against expert-written test cases, requirements or other standards (Syllabus 2.3.1). Diversity, time efficiency and execution success rate measure variety, time saved and executability.' },
  { id:'c2-032', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.1', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Which of the following statements about evaluating GenAI results with metrics are correct?\ni. Metrics can be evaluated by manual reviews or automated, for example by comparing the LLM output against a predefined reference.\nii. In addition to the general metrics, task-specific metrics can be tailored to specific test activities.\niii. Because the metrics are objective, the output of a single run of a prompt is a sufficient basis for them.', opts:['i and iii only', 'i and ii only', 'ii and iii only', 'i, ii and iii'], ans:[1], exp:'Statements i and ii restate Syllabus 2.3.1. Statement iii is wrong: because of the non-deterministic nature of GenAI, metrics must be based on statistically relevant data, not on a single run.' },
  { id:'c2-033', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.2', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'A tester starts with a short base prompt for generating API test cases, reviews the output, then adds details about the authentication rules and replaces ambiguous terms before running the prompt again. Which technique is mainly applied?', opts:['Iterative prompt modification', 'A/B testing of prompts', 'Integrating user feedback', 'Sharing prompt libraries'], ans:[0], exp:'Iterative prompt modification starts with a base prompt and modifies it based on actual results, gradually adding context or adjusting wording to improve specificity and relevance (Syllabus 2.3.2). No parallel versions are compared, no tester survey is involved and nothing is shared.' },
  { id:'c2-034', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.2', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Which description matches A/B testing of prompts?', opts:['Creating several versions of a prompt and evaluating which one produces better results based on predefined metrics', 'Starting with one base prompt and gradually adding context to it after each result', 'Gathering the opinions of testers about the usefulness and clarity of the generated output', 'Examining the generated output for inconsistencies with the test basis'], ans:[0], exp:'A/B testing creates multiple prompt versions and compares them using predefined metrics to find the phrasing or structure with the most accurate and relevant results (Syllabus 2.3.2). The other options describe iterative modification, user feedback and output analysis.' },
  { id:'c2-035', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.2', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'A tester notices that generated test cases repeatedly apply password rules that are not in the specification. By examining the output for inconsistencies with the test basis and identifying the type of error, the tester adds a constraint to the prompt. Which technique is this?', opts:['A/B testing of prompts', 'Output analysis', 'Integrating user feedback', 'Sharing prompt libraries'], ans:[1], exp:'Output analysis examines AI-generated output for inaccuracies or inconsistencies, e.g. with respect to the test basis, and uses the understanding of the error types to refine the prompt (Syllabus 2.3.2). The other techniques involve comparing prompt versions, collecting tester opinions or sharing prompts.' },
  { id:'c2-036', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.2', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'After a sprint, testers report that the generated test cases are too coarse-grained to be executed. The team changes the prompt so that step-level detail is required. Which technique is mainly applied?', opts:['A/B testing of prompts', 'Integrating user feedback', 'Output analysis', 'Zero-shot prompting'], ans:[1], exp:'Integrating user feedback means gathering input from testers about usefulness and clarity of the output, e.g. the level of detail of generated tests, and using it to refine prompts (Syllabus 2.3.2). The change here is driven by tester opinions, not by a comparison of versions or by analyzing errors against the test basis.' },
  { id:'c2-037', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.2', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Which statement about prompt length and specificity is correct?', opts:['Longer prompts always produce better test cases, so every detail should be added', 'Shorter prompts always produce better results because the model needs no context', 'Prompt length has no influence on the output, only the role matters', 'Adding more context sometimes improves the response, but in other cases shorter prompts may generalize better, so both should be tried'], ans:[3], exp:'Syllabus 2.3.2 recommends experimenting with different prompt lengths and levels of detail: more context can help in some cases, while shorter prompts may generalize better in others. The absolute statements in the other options are therefore incorrect.' },
  { id:'c2-038', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.2', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'What is a benefit of sharing prompt libraries across a test team or organization?', opts:['It removes the need to evaluate the generated output', 'It makes the output of the LLM deterministic', 'It makes system prompts unnecessary', 'It helps standardize prompt techniques, maintain consistent quality and avoid repeating errors'], ans:[3], exp:'Sharing practices such as prompt libraries standardizes prompt techniques, keeps quality consistent and lets teams build on collective insights and avoid repeated errors (Syllabus 2.3.2). It does not replace output evaluation, change the non-deterministic behavior of GenAI or replace system prompts.' },
  { id:'c2-039', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.2', k:'K2', type:'multi', q:'Which TWO of the following are techniques for evaluating and iteratively refining prompts? (Choose TWO answers.)', opts:['Keeping the first prompt that gives an acceptable output and never revising it', 'A/B testing of prompts', 'Re-running the identical prompt until one output looks correct', 'Removing all context from the prompt to keep results comparable', 'Integrating user feedback from testers'], ans:[1,4], exp:'A/B testing and the integration of user feedback are listed in Syllabus 2.3.2, together with iterative modification, output analysis and adjusting prompt length and specificity. Never revising a prompt, repeating it until one output looks right and removing context are not refinement techniques.' },
  { id:'c2-040', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.2', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'Which of the following statements about refining prompts are correct?\ni. Iterative prompt modification starts with a base prompt and changes it based on actual results, e.g. by adding context or adjusting terminology.\nii. A/B testing compares versions of a prompt based on predefined metrics.\niii. Output analysis is applied only after a prompt is finalized and cannot influence further prompt changes.', opts:['i and iii only', 'ii and iii only', 'i and ii only', 'i, ii and iii'], ans:[2], exp:'Statements i and ii restate Syllabus 2.3.2. Statement iii is wrong: understanding the types of errors found in the output is used to refine prompts and avoid similar defects in future iterations.' },
  { id:'c2-041', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.2', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'A 900-word prompt for generating test cases for a ticketing API produces rigid, repetitive tests that copy the given examples. The team experiments with a shortened version. Which technique is mainly applied?', opts:['Integrating user feedback', 'Adjusting prompt length and specificity', 'Sharing prompt libraries', 'Output analysis'], ans:[1], exp:'Experimenting with different prompt lengths and levels of detail is the technique named \'adjust prompt length and specificity\'; shorter prompts may yield better generalization in some cases (Syllabus 2.3.2). The scenario involves neither tester opinions, nor sharing prompts, nor analyzing error types against the test basis.' },
  { id:'c2-042', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.3.2', k:'K2', type:'single', q:'A team compares two prompt versions V1 and V2 for a hotel-booking app. Each version is run 30 times, the outputs are rated with predefined metrics such as precision and diversity, and the better version is adopted. Why is running each version many times appropriate?', opts:['Because GenAI is non-deterministic, so metrics need statistically relevant data', 'Because repeated runs make the LLM learn the preferred prompt permanently', 'Because diversity can only be computed across several identical outputs', 'Because user feedback must be collected after every run before a version is adopted'], ans:[0], exp:'A/B testing of prompts relies on predefined metrics (Syllabus 2.3.2), and because of the non-deterministic nature of GenAI these metrics must be based on statistically relevant data (Syllabus 2.3.1). Repetition does not train the model, diversity does not need identical outputs, and feedback after every run is not required.' },
  { id:'c2-043', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.1', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'A test analyst at a hotel web shop wants GenAI to find ambiguities in a user story. Which of the following prompts contains all six components of a structured prompt?', opts:['“Review the user story ‘As a guest, I want to cancel my booking so that I get a refund’ for the hotel web shop and tell me what is unclear. Present the findings as a table with the columns Issue, Quote and Question.”', '“You are a senior test analyst for a hotel web shop. Find ambiguities in the user story below and explain each one. User story: ‘As a guest, I want to cancel my booking so that I get a refund.’”', '“You are a senior test analyst. The test object is the room-booking function of a hotel web shop. Identify ambiguities in the user story below. User story: ‘As a guest, I want to cancel my booking so that I get a refund.’ Do not propose new requirements. Present the findings as a table with the columns Issue, Quote and Question.”', '“You are a senior test analyst working on the room-booking function of a hotel web shop. Identify ambiguities in the cancellation user story. Do not propose new requirements. Present the findings as a table with the columns Issue, Quote and Question.”'], ans:[2], exp:'The third option contains role, context, instruction, input data (the story), constraints (no new requirements) and output format (table) (Syllabus 2.1.1, applied in 2.2.1). The first lacks role and constraints, the second lacks constraints and output format, and the last never supplies the user story, so input data is missing.' },
  { id:'c2-044', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.1', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'A tester wants to refine the acceptance criteria of a mobile banking user story with prompt chaining and human verification. Which approach is most appropriate?', opts:['Send one long prompt asking for ambiguities, testability and completeness together and verify only the final answer', 'Prompt one: identify ambiguities; prompt two: evaluate testability; prompt three: evaluate completeness; the tester verifies and corrects the output of each step before continuing', 'Prompt one: evaluate completeness; prompt two: identify ambiguities; prompt three: evaluate testability; the tester verifies only the last output', 'Prompt one: evaluate testability; prompt two: evaluate completeness; prompt three: identify ambiguities; the tester verifies only the first output'], ans:[1], exp:'Prompt chaining with human verification (Syllabus 2.1.2, 2.2.5) splits the work into steps and has the tester verify and correct the output of each step so the next step starts from a clean result. Analyzing ambiguities, then testability, then completeness is the logical order for refining acceptance criteria. A single prompt is not a chain, and verifying only one output skips step-wise verification.' },
  { id:'c2-045', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.1', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'A tester has 40 test conditions for a loan-application portal and uses the prompt “Prioritize these test conditions.” The LLM returns an arbitrary ranking. Which improvement is most appropriate?', opts:['Ask the LLM to estimate the risk of every condition without supplying project data, to keep the result unbiased', 'Provide the likelihood and impact of failure for each condition, plus relevant information such as regulatory relevance, and ask for a priority level per condition', 'Add only the role “You are a test manager” and keep the rest of the prompt unchanged', 'Ask the LLM to sort the conditions by their order of appearance in the requirements document'], ans:[1], exp:'With information on risk likelihood and impact (and aspects such as regulatory compliance or defect history) an LLM can recommend priority levels (Syllabus 2.2.1); the quality of the input data directly affects the output. Without risk data the ranking stays arbitrary, a role alone adds no data, and document order is not a risk-based priority.' },
  { id:'c2-046', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.1', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'A tester has a user story and a GUI wireframe of the payment page of an online pharmacy and wants GenAI to derive acceptance criteria. Which approach is most appropriate?', opts:['Send a structured multimodal prompt containing the story text and the wireframe image as input data, plus constraints such as field rules and business rules', 'Send only the wireframe image and ask for all acceptance criteria, because an image contains all necessary information', 'Describe the wireframe in one sentence and omit the story to keep the prompt short', 'Send only the user story text, because LLMs cannot use images as input data'], ans:[0], exp:'A user story and a GUI wireframe are both input data for test analysis (Syllabus 2.1.1 lists screenshots as input data and 2.2.1 lists GUI wireframes), and a structured prompt adds constraints such as field rules and business rules. Using only one source loses information, and the claim that LLMs cannot use images contradicts multimodal LLMs (1.1.4).' },
  { id:'c2-047', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.2', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'A team wants GenAI to turn user stories into Gherkin scenarios that always follow the team\'s established wording and structure. Which prompt is most appropriate?', opts:['“Write good Gherkin scenarios for the user story below and be consistent with our team style.”', '“Tell me which Gherkin style is most common and use that style for the user story below.”', '“Split the story into three prompts, one that writes all Given steps, one for all When steps and one for all Then steps.”', '“Here are two examples, each with a user story, its test conditions and the resulting Given-When-Then scenarios. Create scenarios in the same style for this new user story: …”'], ans:[3], exp:'Few-shot prompting suits tasks with a specific pattern such as Gherkin test cases: examples with story, test conditions and scenarios model the expected result (Syllabus 2.2.2 and 2.2.5). Without examples the \'team style\' is unknown to the LLM, the popular style is not the team style, and splitting by keyword adds steps without conveying the style.' },
  { id:'c2-048', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.2', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'A tester wants functional test cases for a user story and, afterwards, end-to-end test procedures. Which sequence is most appropriate?', opts:['Use a meta prompt for end-to-end procedures first; then generate the functional test cases; finally check coverage of the criteria', 'Produce the coverage table first; then generate the test cases from the acceptance criteria; finally use a meta prompt for end-to-end procedures', 'Generate test cases from the acceptance criteria in a defined output format; have the LLM produce a table checking that every criterion is covered; then use a meta prompt to help create the prompt for end-to-end procedures', 'Generate the test cases; use a meta prompt for end-to-end procedures; check the coverage of the acceptance criteria only at the very end'], ans:[2], exp:'Prompt chaining means each result is checked before the next step starts (Syllabus 2.1.2, 2.2.5). The test cases come first (2.2.2), then their completeness is verified with a coverage table, and only then is a meta prompt used to help create the prompt for end-to-end procedures. A coverage table cannot precede the test cases, and creating the end-to-end prompt before the functional tests are verified, or checking only at the very end, skips the verification step of the chain.' },
  { id:'c2-049', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.2', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'A tester must order 60 regression test cases of an e-commerce checkout by risk and by dependencies (for example, “create account” must run before “save address”). Which approach is most appropriate?', opts:['Use one prompt containing only the test case titles and trust the resulting order, since the LLM infers dependencies reliably', 'Ask for the final schedule in one step and skip verification, because multi-criteria reasoning is a core strength of LLMs', 'Use prompt chaining: one prompt prioritizes by risk, the next adds the dependencies, and the tester manually verifies each intermediate result for reasoning errors', 'Ask the LLM to ignore dependencies, since only risk matters for the execution order'], ans:[2], exp:'Prioritizing by several criteria such as risk and dependencies needs precision with human verification at each step, which is what prompt chaining offers (Syllabus 2.2.2, 2.2.5, 2.1.2); LLM reasoning in such tasks can contain errors (3.1.1). Titles alone do not convey dependencies, skipping verification contradicts the syllabus, and ignoring dependencies would produce an unexecutable order.' },
  { id:'c2-050', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.2', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'A tester of an insurance portal needs test data for claim forms, including extreme situations, without exposing any customer information. Which prompt is most appropriate?', opts:['“You are a test data engineer. Generate 30 synthetic claim records that resemble production data in structure and value ranges, including extreme cases such as the maximum claim amount. Do not use real customer data. Output as CSV.”', '“Here are 30 real customer claim records. Copy them and change one field in each record.”', '“Generate only typical claim records with average amounts so that the test data looks realistic.”', '“Generate test data for the insurance portal.”'], ans:[0], exp:'GenAI can create representative, privacy-preserving synthetic test data that resembles production data and covers extreme situations without exposing sensitive information (Syllabus 2.2.2). Pasting real records exposes customer data, typical-only data misses extreme situations, and the last prompt gives no context, constraints or format.' },
  { id:'c2-051', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.3', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'An automation engineer has a keyword library for a web banking app (OpenApp, EnterText, ClickButton, VerifyText) and 50 manual test cases. Which prompt is most appropriate to generate keyword-driven scripts in the team\'s format?', opts:['“Write automated tests in Python for the following 50 manual test cases of a web banking app.”', '“Invent the keywords you find most convenient and write scripts for the following manual test case.”', '“Here is a one-line description of the application. Generate scripts for all its test cases.”', '“Here is the keyword library documentation and two examples, each showing a manual test case and its keyword script. Convert the following manual test case in the same way: …”'], ans:[3], exp:'Few-shot prompting suits repetitive tasks with a specific pattern such as keyword-driven scripts, and the LLM maps the predefined keywords to test cases (Syllabus 2.2.3 and 2.2.5). The other prompts neither provide the keyword library nor examples of the expected script format, and an LLM-invented keyword set would not match the framework.' },
  { id:'c2-052', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.3', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'A team analyzes the results of a nightly REST API regression run with a chain of prompts in one LLM conversation. Which sequence is most appropriate?', opts:['Cross-check the findings; analyze the results against the specification; cluster similar defects; maintain the list of known anomalies', 'Analyze the results and compare them with the test specification; cluster similar defects; maintain the list of known anomalies; cross-check the findings', 'Cluster similar defects; analyze the results against the specification; cross-check the findings; maintain the list of known anomalies', 'Maintain the list of known anomalies; cluster similar defects; cross-check the findings; analyze the results against the specification'], ans:[1], exp:'Prompt chaining breaks a task into steps whose results are checked before the next step (Syllabus 2.1.2, 2.2.5). In regression test report analysis the results must first be analyzed and compared with the test specification; only then can similar defects be clustered, the list of known anomalies maintained and the findings cross-checked, all within one conversation (2.2.3). The other orders put steps before the results they depend on.' },
  { id:'c2-053', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.3', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'After a change to the discount module of a web shop, the CI pipeline would run 3,000 regression tests in four hours. A tester wants GenAI to focus the regression effort. Which prompt is most appropriate?', opts:['“Here is the list of regression test IDs. Tell me which tests are likely to fail in the next run.”', '“Summarize all 3,000 regression tests and run them in alphabetical order.”', '“Remove every regression test that passed in the last ten runs from the suite.”', '“You are a test automation engineer. Here is the code diff of the discount module and the list of regression tests with the functions they cover. Identify high-risk areas affected by the change and rank the tests to run first, with a short justification.”'], ans:[3], exp:'GenAI can analyze code changes to identify high-risk areas and so enable targeted regression testing (impact analysis and test optimization, Syllabus 2.2.3). The correct prompt supplies the change and the coverage information as input data; the others give no change information, ignore impact analysis or delete tests without a change-based rationale. The output must still be checked.' },
  { id:'c2-054', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.3', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'GUI regression tests for a travel web shop fail after each release because element locators change and a confirmation dialog was added, although the functionality is unchanged. Which use of GenAI is most appropriate?', opts:['Give GenAI the failing scripts, error logs and the changed page structure so that it adapts locators and interactions, then review the updated scripts before adding them to the suite', 'Ask GenAI to mark the failing tests as passed in the next test report', 'Ask GenAI to delete the failing GUI tests and rely only on API tests', 'Ask GenAI to regenerate the whole suite from scratch and add it without review'], ans:[0], exp:'Self-healing and adaptive tests adjust scripts to minor UI changes such as dynamic locators and modified interactions, but the generated output must be carefully checked depending on risk (Syllabus 2.2.3). Falsifying results, deleting tests or accepting unreviewed output does not address the cause.' },
  { id:'c2-055', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.4', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'A test manager wants a stakeholder-friendly summary of test progress for a patient-portal release using an export from the test management tool. Which prompt contains all six components of a structured prompt?', opts:['“You are a test manager. Release 4.2 of a patient portal is two days before the planned end of system testing. Summarize progress and risks from the data in our test management tool. Base statements only on that data and mark assumptions. Write five bullet points in plain language.”', '“Release 4.2 of a patient portal is two days before the planned end of system testing. Summarize progress and risks using the export below. Export: [executed, passed, failed and blocked tests per area; open defects by severity]. Write five bullet points in plain language.”', '“You are a test manager. Release 4.2 of a patient portal is two days before the planned end of system testing. Summarize progress and risks using the export below. Export: [executed, passed, failed and blocked tests per area; open defects by severity]. Base statements only on the export and mark assumptions. Write five bullet points in plain language.”', '“You are a test manager. Summarize progress and risks using the export below. Export: [executed, passed, failed and blocked tests per area; open defects by severity]. Base statements only on the export and mark assumptions.”'], ans:[2], exp:'The third prompt has role, context, instruction, input data (the export), constraints and output format (five plain-language bullets) (Syllabus 2.1.1, applied to 2.2.4). The first does not supply the data itself, the second lacks role and constraints, and the last lacks context and output format.' },
  { id:'c2-056', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.4', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'A test lead types “How is our testing going?” into a GenAI chat and gets a generic answer. The lead needs insights for reprioritizing tests and reallocating testers for the last week of the sprint. Which improvement is most appropriate?', opts:['Provide the current test data (executed and failed tests, defect trends, remaining effort per area), state the control goals and specify the output format, for example a table of recommended changes', 'Ask the same question several times and use the most frequent answer', 'Add the phrase “Answer as an expert” and keep the question otherwise unchanged', 'First ask for a long essay about the theory of test control and then repeat the question'], ans:[0], exp:'GenAI can support test monitoring and control by analyzing test data and giving insights for reprioritizing tests and reallocating resources (Syllabus 2.2.4); this requires the actual data as input and a clear goal and output specification (Syllabus 2.1.1). Repeating a data-free question, adding a vague role or requesting theory does not supply that information.' },
  { id:'c2-057', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.4', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'At the end of a project, a test manager wants GenAI to draft a test completion report that highlights successes and lessons learned. Which prompt is most appropriate?', opts:['“Write a test completion report.”', '“Write a test completion report for a typical project, using your general knowledge about common lessons learned.”', '“You are a test manager. Using the final test results, defect statistics and retrospective notes below, write a test completion report with the sections Achievements, Lessons learned and Improvements for the next release. [data]”', '“Predict the defects that customers will find in production and omit the test results.”'], ans:[2], exp:'GenAI can generate test completion reports that highlight successes and lessons learned (Syllabus 2.2.4). The correct prompt supplies role, project data and the required structure. The other prompts give no project-specific input or ask for generic or unrelated content, which would not reflect the actual project.' },
  { id:'c2-058', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.5', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'A team must produce about 300 test report sections, each in a fixed company layout (heading, status icon and a three-sentence summary). Which prompting technique is most suitable?', opts:['Meta prompting, because the layout is not yet defined and the LLM should invent it', 'Prompt chaining with human verification of every single intermediate sentence', 'Zero-shot prompting, because the LLM already knows the company\'s layout', 'Few-shot prompting, with examples of finished sections in the required layout'], ans:[3], exp:'Few-shot prompting is recommended for repetitive tasks or tasks with a specific or constrained output format, such as test reports with a defined format (Syllabus 2.2.5). The layout is already defined, so meta prompting is unnecessary, chaining with verification of every sentence is unsuited to a repetitive pattern task, and a zero-shot LLM cannot know a company-specific layout.' },
  { id:'c2-059', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.5', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'A tester must analyze the logs of a newly introduced message broker for anomalies, has no experience with prompts for this task and cannot describe the exact steps in advance. Which technique is the best first choice?', opts:['Meta prompting: describe the objective and the task generally and let the LLM draft the prompt, then evaluate and refine it', 'Few-shot prompting with ten worked examples of anomalies, even though none exist yet', 'Zero-shot prompting with the one-word prompt “anomalies”', 'Prompt chaining, because prompts for new tasks can only be written step by step by the tester'], ans:[0], exp:'Meta prompting suits flexible, dynamic tasks and the crafting of prompts for new tasks, such as anomaly detection, with the LLM guided by a general description of objective and task (Syllabus 2.2.5). No examples exist for few-shot, a one-word prompt gives no guidance, and chaining does not require that the tester writes every prompt alone.' },
  { id:'c2-060', ch:2, lo:'GenAI-2.2.5', k:'K3', type:'single', q:'A team will generate keyword-driven API test scripts for a new service. No prompt exists yet, the scripts must follow a fixed pattern, and each part should be validated before the next. Which combination of techniques is most appropriate?', opts:['Few-shot prompting alone, because combining techniques is not possible for one use case', 'Zero-shot prompting combined with chaining, because examples would only restrict the LLM', 'Meta prompting alone, using the generated prompt unchanged and without checking intermediate results', 'Meta prompting to create an initial prompt, adapting its examples (few-shot), and splitting the work into validated subtasks (chaining)'], ans:[3], exp:'Syllabus 2.2.5 states that several techniques can be combined for one use case: meta prompting creates an initial prompt, its examples can be adapted and enhanced (few-shot), and dividing the task into subtasks enables validation of intermediate steps (chaining). The other options exclude techniques the scenario needs or skip validation.' }
);
